/* 
Given the root of a binary tree, determine if it is a valid binary search tree (BST).

A valid BST is defined as follows:

The left subtree of a node contains only nodes with keys strictly less than the node's key.
The right subtree of a node contains only nodes with keys strictly greater than the node's key.
Both the left and right subtrees must also be binary search trees.
*/

Start from the root node of the binary tree.



Check whether the current node is within the allowed minimum and maximum values.



If the node is less than or equal to the minimum or greater than or equal to the maximum, return false.



Recursively check the left subtree, updating the maximum value to the current node's value.



Recursively check the right subtree, updating the minimum value to the current node's value.



If both subtrees satisfy the BST properties, return true; otherwise, return false.
